    solution: "<h2>Context</h2><p>The credential system needs to prove that a person's ID was checked, but scanned documents contain personal data that privacy law says must be protectable and deletable.</p><h2>Options</h2><ul><li>Store the scans on-chain, which is public and permanent.</li><li>Store them on IPFS, which is public and hard to delete.</li><li>Keep them in a regulated off-chain store and record only a hash and a verification status on-chain.</li></ul><h2>Decision</h2><p>We will use the regulated off-chain store and put only the hash and the verified flag on the chain, because the chain cannot delete data, so personal data must not be there.</p><h2>Consequences</h2><p>Privacy rules can be followed, because a record can be deleted from the store while the chain keeps only a meaningless fingerprint. The system now depends on the off-chain store being available and secure, so it needs backups, access controls and audits.</p>",
