    solution: "<h1>DegreeChain</h1><h2>Goals</h2><p>Let any employer verify a graduate's degree within seconds, without contacting the university, while keeping personal data private and letting universities revoke wrong certificates.</p><h2>Network type</h2><p>A permissioned consortium network run by the state's universities and the education department, each operating a validator node. Employers read through public gateways. Membership needs approval from the department.</p><h2>Data placement</h2><p>On-chain: the hash of each certificate, the issuing university's identity, the issue date and a revoked flag. Off-chain: the certificate PDF and the student's personal data, which stay with the university and the student. A verifier hashes the document they receive and compares it with the on-chain hash.</p><h2>Scaling</h2><p>The state issues about 300,000 certificates a year, which is under one a second on average, so a consortium chain with a few hundred transactions per second is ample. Certificates are written in batches, and a Merkle root per batch keeps writes small.</p><h2>Security and keys</h2><p>Each university signs with a key held in a hardware security module and two officers must approve each batch. Keys rotate every year, and a lost key can be replaced through a decision of the consortium board.</p><h2>Operations</h2><p>Three nodes per university across two sites, with monitoring, daily backups and a documented upgrade process that needs approval of two thirds of members. A public status page shows uptime.</p><h2>Risks</h2><ul><li>An insider at a university issues a fake certificate, so every issuance is logged and audited.</li><li>Private data leaks from an off-chain store, so access controls and encryption are mandatory.</li><li>A single point of failure at the department, so governance is shared and nodes are spread across institutions.</li></ul>",
